{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310635,"nonce":"//5RJsSFAgz44JnUqcVokbujTYs=","title":"2026-07-14 ROOT-FIXED two cross-module interface bugs the disease cluster kept re-hitting. (A) derived-interface dispatch (I extends Base) ...","message":"**The user is tired of recurring cross-module bugs and asked why FQN doesn't already\nsolve them.** Answer: the compiler HAS name/FQN-tolerant resolvers, but they are applied\nINCONSISTENTLY — many sites still resolve cross-module references by **SymbolId** (a global\nmonotonic counter that DRIFTS across compile contexts; the forwarded maps keep the\n*originating* context's ids). Every site that does a raw `.get(\u0026sym)` instead of a\nname-match is a latent cross-module bug. This is the root of [[bugs_xmodule_resolution_disease_cluster]].\n\nInterface values are inline fat pointers `{obj@0, fn0@8, fn1@16, ...}`; a method's slot =\nits position in `interface_method_names[iface]` (built `[base methods..., own methods...]`).\n`register_interface_metadata` merged the parent (base) methods via a raw\n`interface_method_names.get(\u0026parent_sym)` (hir_to_mir.rs ~39609) — on SymbolId drift it\nMISSED, so a derived interface (`Attention extends Module`) lost its base methods in the\ntruncating context. The fat-ptr builder (name-tolerant `resolve_interface_method_names`)\nand the call site (raw `.get`) then disagreed on the slot -\u003e `forwardMasked` read a fn-ptr\nPAST the malloc'd fat pointer -\u003e SIGSEGV **before entering the method**. The downcast\n`cast(attn, MHA)` crashed for the same reason (its offset-0 obj recovery needs a well-formed\nfat ptr). **Fix:** route the parent-merge (via `get_interface_symbol` for Placeholder\nparents + `resolve_interface_method_names`), both dispatch call sites (10218/13019), and the\neager vtable builder (39196) through the name-tolerant resolver; make\n`wrap_in_interface_fat_ptr` reject a cached eager vtable SHORTER than the resolved method\nlist. Validated: pure-Haxe BERT `MultiHeadAttention.forwardMasked` (Attention extends Module,\nbuilt in nue.arch, dispatched from nue.transformer) now dispatches across all 6 layers\n(was SIGSEGV); single-file 14-scenario + cross-module interface matrices pass; llama clean.\n\n`cond ? new A() : new B()` upcast to a shared interface built ONE fat pointer using the\n**then-branch's** class (A) vtable for both branches. Phi selects the right OBJECT (B) but\nevery method slot points to A's methods -\u003e `t.m2()` returns \"A.m2\" for a B object (silent\nwrong dispatch; `t.getTag()`==222 only because A.getTag/B.getTag are byte-identical). Root:\n`find_common_supertype` (type_inference.rs:234) is a stub returning the FIRST type, so the\nwhole `?:` types as class A; the single downstream `maybe_wrap_for_interface` sees only A.\n**Fix (MIR layer, hir_to_mir.rs `lower_conditional_typed`):** when both branches are\ndifferent concrete classes upcast to a shared interface, wrap EACH branch to\n`shared_interface_for(tc,ec)` (most-derived shared iface) INSIDE its own block so the phi\nmerges two correct fat pointers; mark them + the phi in `interface_wrapped_args` and guard\n`maybe_wrap_for_interface`'s class arm against re-wrapping an already-wrapped value.\n\nThe \"2nd `Array` arg dropped\" symptom was NOT signature resolution. Root: `Array.copy()`\nmapped straight to the void out-param runtime fn `haxe_array_copy(out, arr)` with NO MIR\nwrapper to allocate `out` (slice/join/concat each have a `build_array_*` wrapper; copy was the\nonly one missing it). So `copy()` returned null. `ids2 = ids.copy()` was null -\u003e `embed(ids2,\nmask)`'s null first arg made the arg-collection `filter_map` DROP it, sliding `mask` into `ids`\nand nulling the mask. Fix: added `build_array_copy` (mirrors `build_array_slice`), registered\nit, routed the `copy` mapping to `array_copy` (`returns: primitive`). With it, the BERT\nattention-mask feature works end-to-end: right-padded+masked embedding == unpadded (cosine 1.0).\n**DEBUG LESSON: this hunt was polluted by the stale-cache gotcha ([[feedback_blade_cache_clear_all_rayzor_dirs]])\n— several \"mask null\" / \"throw skipped\" readings were stale. Always `find . -name .rayzor -exec rm -rf`.**\n\n`Factory.callGeneric\u003cT:Base\u003e(x){return x.m();}` called from another module SIGILL'd (W0020). Callee\ncompiles as iface dispatch (x = Base fat ptr); caller passed RAW class because in the importer's\ncontext the param arrives as a `TypeParameter` with an EMPTY constraint list (constraints don't\nsurvive the module boundary). The whole name-based x-module iface-param wrap already exists\n(`external_function_param_iface_names` -\u003e `maybe_materialize_for_call` hir_to_mir.rs ~25548), fed by\n`resolve_name` in the forwarding step (compilation.rs ~5808) — which only handled Class/Interface and\nreturned None for a TypeParameter. Fix = resolve a constrained type param to its interface\nconstraint's NAME there (declaring module, constraint present) so the drift-safe wrap fires. Repro\n`/tmp/cg2`; `\u003cT:Base\u003e` no-method-call and single-file already worked.\n\n`slice(pos,?end)` had one params:2 mapping to the 3-param `array_slice` wrapper. Called with 1 arg it\nbuilt a 2-arg call -\u003e wrapper read garbage `end` -\u003e `end.min(len)` empty. Fix = a params:1 mapping to\nnew `array_slice_to_end` wrapper (passes i64::MAX; runtime clamps to len), keeping params:2 for\nexplicit end. `find_by_name_and_params` dispatches by arg count — the SAME param-count overloading\n`String.indexOf/substr/lastIndexOf` already use (lower-count entry first). NOT fill_default_args.\n\nThe most hostile shape in this family. The slot loop was\n`if let Some(func_id) = func_id_opt { ...store... }` and then returned `Some(fat_ptr)`\n**regardless**. An unresolved method left its slot as *uninitialised malloc bytes*.\n\nWhy that is worse than a null: the wrapper is **non-null**, so the user's own\n`if (x == null) throw` guard PASSES, and the program dies later at a `blr` through\ngarbage — in JIT'd code, no symbol, arbitrarily far from the cause.\n\n**Repro/discovery (nue BERT):** adding `Linear.fromQuant(...)` inside `BertArch.makeLinear`\nmade an UNRELATED `ArchRegistry.build()` SIGSEGV. Spooky-at-a-distance: the new x-module\ndependency pulled a *duplicate, incomplete local copy* of `BertArch` into the ArchRegistry\nmodule (object malloc'd 8 bytes vs the real 16; ctor lowered as local `fn14` instead of\nx-module `fn650010`), so all three `ArchBuilder` slots failed BOTH SymbolId and FQN lookup.\n\n**Method that found it — A/B MIR diff, generalise this:** dump `RAYZOR_DUMP_MIR=1` for the\nworking and crashing variants and diff the SAME function. `@build` was identical except\nuniformly renumbered callees (fn17-\u003efn18...) — a red herring; the real signal was in\n`@withDefaults`: working emitted `fn_ref @fn650033/4/5` + stores at offsets 8/16/24, crashing\nemitted `malloc(32)`, stored the object, and called `register()` with ZERO slots written.\nFaulting insn `ldp x9, x0, [x8]` with x8=0 then `tst w0,#0xff` identified it as the\nBool-returning `arch.validate()` dispatch.\n\n**Fix:** give the loop the same NAME-keyed forward-ref thunk fallback that\n`wrap_new_class_as_interface_by_name` already used (`forward_ref_dispatch_thunk_by_name`,\ndedupes with the real dispatcher at merge); if a slot is truly unnameable, REFUSE the\nwrapper (`return None`) instead of booby-trapping it. Matches [[feedback_no_silent_dispatch_fallthrough]].\nResult: nue BERT loads+embeds on the FULL quant path (QTensor token embedding AND linears),\ndim=384 L2=1.0, dequant workaround deleted. `textgold` still exits 139 = NEXT layer\n(native quant encoder), not a regression — quant BERT never loaded at all before this.\n\nCompanion fixes same session: `clone_interface_fat_ptr` unconditionally dereferenced the source\nwrapper, so `var a:Iface = returnsNull()` SIGSEGV'd on the BIND before any user null check ran\n— added a null-guard branch/phi. And `haxe_vtable_lookup` (runtime/src/type_system.rs) now\nTHROWS a named exception on null receiver / out-of-range slot / unregistered type_id instead\nof returning 0 that every caller immediately `call_indirect`s.\n\n**Rule this establishes:** a partially-built dispatch structure must never escape the builder.\nEither every slot resolves, or construction fails loudly.\n\n- **The \"masked embed inside a for loop SIGSEGV\" is RESOLVED** — root cause was NOT a loop/lifetime\n  bug at all (my earlier InsertFreePass / loop-invariant-array hypothesis here was WRONG). It was an\n  UNFILLED OPTIONAL ARG: `embed(ids, ?mask)` called without `mask` read caller garbage in the x2 ABI\n  slot; the loop just poisoned x2 deterministically. Fixed generally in build_call_direct. Full\n  writeup: [[bugs_optional_arg_unfilled_garbage_abi_slot]].\n- Array.slice NEGATIVE end (`slice(-2)`) not handled (runtime takes usize; would clamp to len not\n  len+end) — separate/minor.\n\n**Durable fix direction (what the user wants):** audit every `interface_*`/method/signature\n`.get(\u0026sym)` that can see a forwarded cross-module SymbolId and route it through a\nname/FQN-tolerant resolver (the existing `resolve_interface_method_names` /\n`get_interface_symbol` pattern). See agent maps in this session's transcript for the full\nsite list (compilation.rs 5783 in-process forwarding keeps originating keys; BLADE restore\n3676 re-keys by name — make the in-process path match).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_interface_dispatch_symbolid_drift_fix` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}